**Day 29: Mini Project – Student Records DB**  

**Topic:**  
Design and implement a Student Records database using MySQL 8, focusing on schema design, triggers, views, and stored procedures.  

**Learning Objectives:**  

• Reinforce schema design principles for a student records system  
• Implement triggers to automate grade calculations  
• Create views for simplified data access (e.g., marks)  
• Develop stored procedures for enrollment management  

**Activities / Deliverables:**  

• Design and build a Student Records database schema  
• Create an enrollment stored procedure (`enrollment_SP`)  
• Develop a marks view for easy student performance tracking  
• Implement a grade trigger to auto-calculate grades based on marks  

**Important Concepts:**  

• Schema normalization for efficient data storage  
• Trigger automation for business logic (e.g., grading)  
• Views for abstracting complex queries (e.g., marks aggregation)  

**SQL Syntax / Commands:**  

• `CREATE TRIGGER grade_trigger AFTER INSERT ON marks FOR EACH ROW ...`  
• `CREATE VIEW marks_view AS SELECT student_id, subject, score FROM ...`  
• `CALL enrollment_SP(student_id, course_id, enrollment_date);`  

**Real-world Use Cases:**  

• Universities automating student grade management  
• Schools tracking enrollment and academic performance  
• EdTech platforms generating reports via views  

**Tools / Tech:**  

• MySQL 8.0  
• MySQL Workbench for schema design and query execution  

**Summary:**  
Today’s project reinforced core DBA skills by building a functional Student Records DB. Key takeaways include schema optimization, trigger-driven automation, and simplifying data access with views and stored procedures.  

